#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059519 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4af25d77-e2aa-585a-971f-693c44f34e48" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Variable in One Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "935e8644-8d66-5be6-87ec-b8176675aa91" :question/math-academy-id "e-4233" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 23 = \\dfrac 2x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 23 = \\dfrac 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 32 = \\dfrac x2\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n![](images/8e/8e7b2d027a761ba93f679cfb517709811938da412b06e59212391741b5660a2b.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059520 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93665eb6-5d3e-52d2-afc6-bc27488190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial in One Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "e7d11d09-773c-58a9-8582-2cd47a3b141c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4234" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{x+1} = 2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{x+1} = 2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac {x+1}{1} = \\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\dfrac 12 .\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n![](images/d7/d7150c6093121f9f6dc0ecd9675d5be8f0855d339f3404d7a357875362d9990f.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059521 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c24d70-3bde-5277-bc92-ae081065deee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial and a Monomial in Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "3ae175a8-7654-57c8-b5d8-33d7892b7a24" :question/math-academy-id "e-4235" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{x-2} = \\dfrac 3{5x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero, and\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{x-2} = \\dfrac 3{5x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac {x-2}{1} = \\dfrac{5x}{3}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to \n\n$$\nx-2 = \\dfrac{5x}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot (x-2) & =3\\cdot \\frac{5x}{3} \\\\ 3x-6 & =5x \\\\ 3x-5x & =6 \\\\ -2x & =6 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059522 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387a794f-17ea-5ea9-86a1-656a495f62cb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with Binomials in Both Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "13f9fd01-802e-5331-b422-b9aa2cdda7ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-4236" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{2x-1} = \\dfrac{1}{x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=\\dfrac 12$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero, and\n\n- $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{2x-1} = \\dfrac{1}{x+1} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{2x-1}{1} = \\dfrac{x+1}1\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n2x-1 = x+1.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =x+1 \\\\ 2x-x & =1+1 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387a794f-17ea-5ea9-86a1-656a495f62cb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with Binomials in Both Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30bc81e6-8fff-54e2-ac15-49e3fb530116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503070} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff51bff-7de4-5253-9d95-cedff81705fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503245} {:answer/id #uuid "8d0aa041-1831-5a03-87fe-4c02c07d7e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503266} {:answer/id #uuid "a0dc91f3-4ee3-57a4-8547-157373bab8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503304} {:answer/id #uuid "d8fd723a-f248-5a39-96ba-76e1a1a6935a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ff51bff-7de4-5253-9d95-cedff81705fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503245} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c930558-3f13-59ed-984d-aa81dfac2ac2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e3eaae0-125c-585a-90f2-bbc8b4da35cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-106354" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{x+5}=\\frac{3}{2x+12}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-5$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=-6$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+5}{1}=\\frac{2x+12}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+15}{3}=\\frac{2x+12}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot (\\frac{3x+15}{3}) & =3\\cdot (\\frac{2x+12}{3}) \\\\ 3x+15 & =2x+12 \\\\ 3x-2x & =12-15 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4af25d77-e2aa-585a-971f-693c44f34e48" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Variable in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dddef715-15d1-5688-9498-02a18f5a82ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058849} {:answer/id #uuid "1bbfc72d-60dc-598a-9e50-9f1f78d315f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058850} {:answer/id #uuid "2b4db2de-8f65-5588-b118-1aba6c4f87a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058851} {:answer/id #uuid "30236ad8-46b9-5497-9506-c0cfd32bcde6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058852} {:answer/id #uuid "785d1c3c-d3a1-5646-8df8-6839743174cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "785d1c3c-d3a1-5646-8df8-6839743174cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058853} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5489cf7-7cba-528f-8bd9-9e7c58d401ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eddd7f11-274f-563c-bc35-743113376d12" :question/math-academy-id "q-67325" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-\\frac{1}{2x}=\\frac{3}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2x}{1}=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n-2x=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-2x) & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\cancel{(-\\frac{1}{2})}\\cdot (\\cancel{-2}x) & =-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387a794f-17ea-5ea9-86a1-656a495f62cb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with Binomials in Both Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c71a3a11-3eba-5020-9597-3de35a5ab785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058867} {:answer/id #uuid "c768ab08-49e9-5e5c-928d-98edae762f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058868} {:answer/id #uuid "cf329fcc-fb6f-59d1-adcc-57bc40719b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{42}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058869} {:answer/id #uuid "22218b3e-4939-5119-bb7f-f0028c856a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058870} {:answer/id #uuid "2c81b1e5-f63c-5724-81ac-8400764b9ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22218b3e-4939-5119-bb7f-f0028c856a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058870} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfde7113-aa52-5603-817f-a4c288821339" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b4e7858-1264-5def-86e5-7598a8adf5a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-67329" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{x-3}=\\frac{2}{x+6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=3$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=-6$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{6}=\\frac{x+6}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $6.$ The equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{6}=\\frac{3x+18}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{x-3}{6} & =6\\cdot \\frac{3x+18}{6} \\\\ x-3 & =3x+18 \\\\ x-3x & =18+3 \\\\ -2x & =21 \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{2})(-2x) & =(-\\frac{1}{2})21 \\\\ (-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}})(-\\cancel{2}x) & =(-\\frac{1}{2})21 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{21}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93665eb6-5d3e-52d2-afc6-bc27488190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8be19f3-d1d1-5874-a267-0e4a8dae558d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058885} {:answer/id #uuid "df043132-a831-5ea4-b43a-b3fdb0795ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058886} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d6978c-429a-5678-9673-1dad8790f86f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058887} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d08e45-da77-54d2-b0fb-f110b3c9d317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058888} {:answer/id #uuid "1a59f56e-4565-51e2-8e93-bdc2b248a059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a59f56e-4565-51e2-8e93-bdc2b248a059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058889} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4328795-e359-574d-9185-453cc4f416b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bbc3969-7e62-5072-9fb8-19f0fe79bb27" :question/math-academy-id "q-67355" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{1}{x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{x+1}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}=x+1\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & =x+1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}-1 & =x+1-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4af25d77-e2aa-585a-971f-693c44f34e48" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Variable in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62c728d8-29c1-5f90-a9bc-17eae1a900d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503171} {:answer/id #uuid "7733ee4f-eef5-5fd6-a757-f34e447b3828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503219} {:answer/id #uuid "80d7c6b7-28c9-5bea-bee5-7bb87f769d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503246} {:answer/id #uuid "c044e13e-665f-594c-8087-19c0c493d990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503366} {:answer/id #uuid "da440c13-f203-5583-aa96-40df058f74cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62c728d8-29c1-5f90-a9bc-17eae1a900d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503171} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1354d1c-de34-5200-8c22-d682a6a448d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9423a212-cd5b-5be8-a581-5c447b673232" :question/math-academy-id "q-67407" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{4}{3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{3x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & =\\frac{3x}{4} \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4} & =4\\cdot \\frac{3x}{4} \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\cancel{4}} & =\\cancel{4}\\cdot \\frac{3x}{\\cancel{4}} \\\\ 5 & =3x \\\\ (\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 5 & =(\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot (3x) \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & =\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}\\cdot (\\cancel{3}x) \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=\\frac{5}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93665eb6-5d3e-52d2-afc6-bc27488190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d3d307-80c5-5100-8287-638a2bdecc60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502991} {:answer/id #uuid "0cd1f877-23e8-59f2-a2aa-0744c5e7dd70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503013} {:answer/id #uuid "33f90d44-4628-5b5e-b9c2-d33e90471828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503075} {:answer/id #uuid "5ef03d2c-7e33-54bd-ac16-3e50ecf310c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503166} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd6e3e2-7e5b-5a15-8a25-1e78f2e97115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebd6e3e2-7e5b-5a15-8a25-1e78f2e97115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503463} :answer-field/id #uuid "44ae6554-557c-5337-b681-7691c583fdd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "528cc092-636b-5bd7-9846-07908de9113d" :question/math-academy-id "q-67416" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{x-2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication and the addition principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{2}) & =2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ (\\cancel{2})\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{\\cancel{2}}) & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ x-2 & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-2+2 & =\\frac{1}{2}+2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93665eb6-5d3e-52d2-afc6-bc27488190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095c670a-25a7-50cf-a6e1-36f32e4baf89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503001} {:answer/id #uuid "709e927c-126c-5ca0-b3f5-2687d69d3389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503208} {:answer/id #uuid "7c467b23-4da6-5c03-a3aa-f8f4d7260107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503234} {:answer/id #uuid "b34d2f08-dbcb-50dc-b729-b8924b43ec51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503346} {:answer/id #uuid "e7cd306d-6fe0-5878-8f56-d8dc223f7387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "095c670a-25a7-50cf-a6e1-36f32e4baf89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503001} :answer-field/id #uuid "62a98138-ae00-5cf7-a233-4b12748aeb11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f68ddcf-7f49-5e13-8fb1-2db21d4acf5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-67419" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{1}{x+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=-3$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{x+3}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}=x+3\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =x+3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}-3 & =x+3-3 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c24d70-3bde-5277-bc92-ae081065deee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial and a Monomial in Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e09809f-1ac1-5163-95fe-56a6c3cca096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503015} {:answer/id #uuid "1702597c-5660-544c-a89c-cba9f00560d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503028} {:answer/id #uuid "2f7989a1-6ce6-581c-a185-11e1a6852423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503069} {:answer/id #uuid "78dcdf47-9629-5ab8-a15d-6bafe37b89e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503225} {:answer/id #uuid "94f3bb0d-e212-55c1-a64b-282f04cc5137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f7989a1-6ce6-581c-a185-11e1a6852423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503069} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4d1887c-0c5d-5ac5-9c0f-77c952d10182" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b01bcc83-5d54-554e-8eb4-318389e1d142" :question/math-academy-id "q-67422" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{x+2}=\\frac{3}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{6}=\\frac{x}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $6.$ The above equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{6}=\\frac{2x}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{6} & =6\\cdot \\frac{2x}{6} \\\\ x+2 & =2x \\\\ 2 & =2x-x \\\\ 2 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c24d70-3bde-5277-bc92-ae081065deee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial and a Monomial in Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55a6fcca-aad9-5ad1-a121-fdaf665d5aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503145} {:answer/id #uuid "d92f70d4-3067-5353-97b7-e64c25a54de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503415} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb302e9-c203-5981-98a6-1abb4868178a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503427} {:answer/id #uuid "e0db71b8-ad73-5cce-b817-b4e5c4f03d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503441} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb895ed-3b69-53d2-9ba7-f858762d5ad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcb302e9-c203-5981-98a6-1abb4868178a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503427} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbaaab81-294e-5a37-9fb1-b73c2d58a5c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a235d31-7002-5190-8de9-b78ca920ac5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-67425" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-\\frac{3}{x}=\\frac{9}{3x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x}{3}=\\frac{3x-1}{9}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $9.$ The above equation becomes\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3x}{9}=\\frac{3x-1}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9)\\cdot \\frac{3x}{-9} & =(-9)\\cdot \\frac{3x-1}{9} \\\\ 3x & =(-1)\\cdot (3x-1) \\\\ 3x & =1-3x \\\\ 3x+3x & =1-3x+3x \\\\ 6x & =1 \\\\ (\\frac{1}{6})\\cdot 6x & =(\\frac{1}{6})\\cdot 1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
